d2d1: assert area conservation in the ear-clip fallback

The test gap this closes: a subtly wrong triangulation is INVISIBLE. Filled opaquely,
overlapping triangles paint exactly the same pixels as a correct triangulation, so two
wrong versions of d2d_ear_clip_contains passed every harness and were caught only by
watching Premiere's recovery counts collapse -- three app runs for something that
should fail in seconds.

Synthetic geometry cannot close it. Four shape families were tried -- a comb at the
measured failing dimensions, a box with duplicate and exactly-collinear vertices, a fan
converging geometrically on a tangent, and a polygon with a reflex vertex sitting
exactly on a candidate ear's chord -- and a deliberately broken build passes all four.
Their on-edge points are collinear, so they are dropped before containment ever sees
them. Premiere's shapes remain the only ones that discriminate.

So assert the invariant instead of trying to reproduce the input. A correct
triangulation of a simple polygon covers it exactly once, so the emitted triangles must
sum to the polygon's own area: overlap pushes the sum above it, a stranded vertex pulls
it below. Both are silent in pixels and loud in arithmetic. area2 is already computed
for winding, and cross IS twice each face's area, so the check costs one add per face.

It runs on the fallback path only, and -- unlike any harness -- it watches the REAL
geometry in every app. A future change that corrupts the triangulation announces itself
in an ordinary log rather than silently drawing wrong shapes.

Also adds preserved-fixes/harnesses/d2d-earclip-strict.c (neutron repo), which fills at
alpha 0.5 so double-composited pixels are detectable, and keeps the four shapes as
regression cover even though they do not discriminate this particular bug.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

--- a/dlls/d2d1/geometry.c
+++ b/dlls/d2d1/geometry.c
@@ -3002,6 +3002,7 @@
 {
     const struct d2d_figure *figure = NULL;
     size_t i, n = 0, remaining, cursor, stalled;
+    double emitted2 = 0.0;          /* NEUTRON AREA CHECK: twice the area of the faces emitted */
     UINT16 *poly;
     double area2 = 0.0;
     BOOL ccw;
@@ -3182,6 +3183,7 @@
         face->v[0] = poly[prev];
         face->v[1] = poly[cursor];
         face->v[2] = poly[next];
+        emitted2 += fabs(cross);        /* NEUTRON AREA CHECK: cross IS twice this face's area */
 
         memmove(&poly[cursor], &poly[cursor + 1], (remaining - cursor - 1) * sizeof(*poly));
         --remaining;
@@ -3191,10 +3193,35 @@
 
     {
         struct d2d_face *face = &geometry->fill.faces[geometry->fill.face_count++];
+        const D2D1_POINT_2F *fa = &sorted[poly[0]], *fb = &sorted[poly[1]], *fc = &sorted[poly[2]];
 
         face->v[0] = poly[0];
         face->v[1] = poly[1];
         face->v[2] = poly[2];
+        emitted2 += fabs(((double)fb->x - fa->x) * ((double)fc->y - fa->y)
+                       - ((double)fb->y - fa->y) * ((double)fc->x - fa->x));
+    }
+
+    /* 🚨 NEUTRON AREA CHECK -- the invariant that makes this algorithm testable.
+     *
+     * A correct triangulation of a simple polygon covers it exactly once: the emitted triangles
+     * must sum to the polygon's own area. Overlapping triangles push the sum ABOVE it; a stranded
+     * vertex or a skipped region pulls it BELOW. Both are otherwise invisible -- filling opaquely
+     * paints identical pixels either way, which is why two wrong versions of
+     * d2d_ear_clip_contains passed every harness and were caught only by Premiere.
+     *
+     * This runs on the fallback path only (a few hundred times a session) and, unlike a harness,
+     * it also watches the REAL geometry in any app, so a subtly wrong triangulation announces
+     * itself in the log instead of silently drawing a wrong shape. */
+    if (fabs(area2) > 0.0)
+    {
+        double err = fabs(fabs(area2) - emitted2) / fabs(area2);
+
+        if (err > 1e-3)
+            ERR("neutron-earclip: AREA MISMATCH %.4f%% -- polygon %.3f, triangles %.3f, %lu faces "
+                "from %lu vertices. The triangulation does not cover the polygon exactly once.\n",
+                err * 100.0, fabs(area2) / 2.0, emitted2 / 2.0,
+                (long)geometry->fill.face_count, (long)n);
     }
 
     /* ERR for the same reason as the decline above: bounded by CDT failures (a few dozen a session),
